--- a/paper/sections/conclusion.tex
+++ b/paper/sections/conclusion.tex
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

-ThreadGate modulates the residual stream with per-layer gated MLP networks and an auxiliary GAR loss. Training succeeds (TF GAR\,=\,0.50), but autoregressive GAR collapses 20--33$\times$ (exposure bias), confirmed on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ by median). Inference-time steering ($\beta$\,=\,5, GAR 0.727) and scheduled sampling (gap ${\sim}$31$\times$$\to$1.57$\times$, no PPL cost, single seed) both demonstrate attention misallocation---not representation degradation---as the proximal cause.
+\looseness=-1 Auxiliary attention losses converge under teacher forcing (GAR\,=\,0.50) but collapse by an order of magnitude under autoregressive generation on two architectures. A five-way diagnostic comparison identifies exposure bias as a primary contributing factor; $\beta$-steering recovers GAR above the TF baseline, consistent with attention misallocation as a proximal cause (KV cache drift remains untested). Scheduled sampling compresses the gap to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (Qwen, 3-seed mean) and ${\sim}$2.9$\times$ (Llama), generalizing across architectures and seeds; to our knowledge, the first such transfer to auxiliary attention objectives in multi-turn transformers.
diff --git a/paper/sections/discussion.tex b/paper/sections/discussion.tex
index 171f14a..3a6e49d 100644
--- a/paper/sections/discussion.tex
+++ b/paper/sections/discussion.tex
@@ -1,9 +1,5 @@
 \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

-The failure traces to an incompatibility between teacher-forcing and GAR-family auxiliary losses, not to the gating architecture. NTP gradient pressure (7.5--14.7$\times$ the GAR magnitude) pushes gates toward identity (2--7\% attenuation), while inference-time steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation is the proximal cause (\S\ref{subsec:steering}). Split-softmax \citep{li2024measuring} offers a complementary inference-time approach that restructures attention computation rather than applying additive bias.
+\looseness=-1 NTP self-recovers because prediction is the same task at both regimes \citep{he2019exposure}; GAR optimizes a \emph{global property} (attention allocation to goal tokens) with no autoregressive counterpart. The collapse is selective: GAR-ours collapses by an order of magnitude while GAR-Dongre shows negligible shift (${\leq}$1.3$\times$). Per-head analysis confirms selectivity: heads with higher TF GAR shift most under AR ($r$\,=\,0.743; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-kl}). Any auxiliary attention objective targeting distributional properties rather than prediction itself should face the same barrier under teacher forcing.

-\paragraph{Scheduled sampling as training-time mitigation.} A preliminary experiment (single seed) applies scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, linearly mixing teacher-forced and model-generated contexts. Autoregressive GAR-ours increases from 0.016 to 0.318 (20$\times$), compressing the ${\sim}$31$\times$ gap to 1.57$\times$ with negligible PPL change (1.38 vs.\ 1.36). GAR-Dongre also rises (0.019$\to$0.353), suggesting indirect improvement of all-token goal attention. The two paths are complementary: $\beta$\,=\,5 achieves the strongest GAR recovery but incurs 24\% PPL cost (1.43\,$\to$\,1.77); $\beta$\,=\,3 offers a more balanced tradeoff (PPL +9\%). Scheduled sampling incurs no fluency cost but requires retraining. A second seed trained from scratch (rather than resumed from a standard-trained epoch) yields AR GAR\,=\,0.418 (gap 1.20$\times$), versus 5\% seed variance in standard ThreadGate training, suggesting that early scheduled sampling exposure---before the model consolidates teacher-forced attention patterns---may further compress the gap; systematic schedule ablation is deferred.
-
-\paragraph{Instruction retention under steering.} Across 18 system-prompt constraint types, $\beta$\,=\,3 steering increases content constraint compliance by +23.8\,pp (GAR-compliance $r$\,=\,0.200, $p$\,=\,0.007; McNemar $p$\,=\,0.077), providing exploratory evidence for downstream benefit pending multi-model confirmation.
-
-TRC analysis (\S\ref{subsec:mechanism-specificity}) confirms the mismatch targets attention routing without disrupting hidden-state coherence. We scope claims to GAR losses on Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B; magnitude imbalance \citep{chen2018gradnorm} and Nash equilibria \citep{navon2022nash} remain untested. Remaining directions include adaptive $\beta$ scheduling and layer-selective steering.
+\looseness=-1 Our exclusion identifies exposure bias as a primary contributing factor, but cannot fully rule out KV cache drift (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). The PCGrad exclusion rules out direction conflict but not magnitude imbalance (7.5--25.7$\times$ NTP dominance; \citealp{chen2018gradnorm}). The GAR--compliance correlation is weak ($r$\,=\,0.200; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ir}), reinforcing GAR's role as a diagnostic probe. Our $\beta$-steering is likewise a \emph{diagnostic}, distinct from inference-time remedies such as split-softmax \citep{li2024measuring} and Rhea \citep{hong2025rhea}. SS and inference-time fixes are complementary: SS modifies representations without inference cost; inference-time methods cannot recover patterns never learned. At $\beta$\,=\,5 the two converge (SS\,=\,0.717 vs.\ TG\,=\,0.727).
